# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MixOS — Docker local development environment
#
# EVERY VALUE IN THIS FILE IS PUBLIC. It is committed to git on purpose, so a
# fresh clone can run `npm run dev:docker` without generating a single secret by
# hand. Treat it as sample data, not as credentials:
#
#   - NEVER copy any value here into a Vercel environment variable, a staging
#     environment, or anything else reachable from the internet. Anyone who has
#     the repo has these keys.
#   - The app is published on 127.0.0.1 ONLY. docker-compose.yml pins the host
#     IP on both app services (`127.0.0.1:3000:3000` on app-dev and on app),
#     so the admin account these credentials unlock is not reachable from the
#     network you happen to be on. That binding is the whole reason committing
#     an AUTH_SECRET, a CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY and a fixed admin password is
#     acceptable — do not drop the host IP, and do not add a second publish of
#     port 3000 without one.
#
# WHAT IS *NOT* LOOPBACK-BOUND: the Supabase CLI's own containers. Verified by
# `docker inspect` against a running CLI 2.115.0 stack — 54321 (Kong/PostgREST),
# 54322 (Postgres) and 54323 (Studio) each report HostIp "", i.e. 0.0.0.0, every
# interface. This is not something we chose or can undo from here: the CLI's
# embedded config schema gives [api], [db] and [studio] a `port` and no
# bind-address key of any spelling, and `supabase start` has no flag for it, so
# supabase/config.toml cannot force loopback in this version. Do not "fix" it by
# inventing a key: an unrecognised field makes every CLI command die with
# `failed to read config: ProjectConfigParseError` (tried it), so a guess here
# takes the whole local stack down rather than being quietly ignored.
#
# The practical consequence, stated plainly because the reader is deciding
# whether these committed secrets are harmless: on an untrusted network someone
# can open Studio on 54323 and connect to Postgres on 54322, which is where
# connector credentials live — encrypted, but with the key printed below. The
# macOS Application Firewall would block that, and it ships DISABLED; Docker
# Desktop also asks you to allow com.docker.backend through it. So "I tried it
# from my phone and it didn't work" is luck, not a control. Run this stack on a
# network you trust, or turn the firewall on. It is a local development
# environment, not a hardened one.
#
# The values are FIXED rather than generated at boot, and that is load-bearing
# in one place: CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY is the AES-256-GCM key for connector
# credentials stored in Postgres (src/lib/connectors/encryption.ts). Rotating it
# would make every previously saved local connector credential undecryptable, so
# a per-boot random key would silently break connectors on every restart.
#
# Loaded by docker-compose via `env_file`, BEFORE .env.docker.local. Note that
# Next.js never auto-loads this file — @next/env only reads .env, .env.local,
# and .env.$NODE_ENV variants — which is exactly why compose passes it as real
# process env instead. Real process env also wins over any .env.local a
# developer has lying around, so the two cannot conflict.
#
# NOT here, deliberately: SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, DATABASE_URL.
# scripts/docker-up.ts reads those from `supabase status -o json` at boot and
# writes them to .env.docker.local (gitignored). The Supabase CLI's local keys
# change format between versions, and a stale hardcoded copy fails as an opaque
# 401 from PostgREST rather than as an obvious misconfiguration.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Auth (NextAuth / Auth.js) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# base64 of 32 random bytes, per the recipe in .env.local.example.
AUTH_SECRET=KAsX07qDL1+yxVyUyIhtDmK5yfkfBumMKCe/r3k6Hhk=

# Must be `localhost`, not `127.0.0.1`. Next 16's dev server 403s any /_next/*
# request — including the HMR websocket — whose Origin is not on its allowlist,
# and that list contains `localhost` but not the loopback IP. Browsing the
# containerized dev server at http://127.0.0.1:3000 gives you a page with every
# chunk unauthorized. (See allowedDevOrigins in the Next docs if that has to
# change.) It also has to match what the browser types, because NEXTAUTH_URL is
# what OAuth redirect URIs and the MCP resource identifier are built from.
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 32 random bytes as hex. Read with Buffer.from(hex, "hex") and handed straight
# to aes-256-gcm, which requires exactly 32 bytes — 64 hex characters, no more,
# no less. Also the HMAC base key for MCP OAuth code/token digests
# (src/lib/mcp/oauth.ts) and the fallback for unlock-cookie signing.
CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY=bc88e1019e4f602ac40eb987afb905e7dd726d6fd0e20f3431d04cec602f06fa

# HMAC key for app password-unlock cookies. Unlike the key above this one is
# used as raw UTF-8 bytes, not decoded from hex, and src/lib/apps/unlock-secret.ts
# ignores it unless it is at least 32 characters — 64 hex chars clears that bar
# with room to spare. Set explicitly (rather than letting it derive from
# CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY) so local behaviour matches a production deployment
# that pins both.
APP_UNLOCK_SECRET=d11ad94e1f40ecfc1e9cc8ebac8b010d06f50c9576b9a9079211c000c7de8ba3

# Bearer token the cron sidecar sends to GET /api/cron, byte-for-byte the way
# Vercel Cron does in production. src/app/api/cron/route.ts refuses to run when
# this is unset rather than defaulting open, so an unset value means background
# jobs silently never fire.
CRON_SECRET=2db2760aed30c647a1c128ca0d6e6eabc2f2981ca0c2dbb4bc92dee33f496ec8

# ── Postgres ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MANDATORY. src/lib/db/pg-options.ts treats an unset PGSSLMODE as `require`,
# on the reasoning that the database is normally a hosted service across the
# public internet. The Supabase CLI's local Postgres speaks plaintext only, so
# without this line every single query fails at the TLS handshake — including
# /api/health/ready, the admin seeder, and the app migration runner.
PGSSLMODE=disable

# ── First-run admin ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Consumed by `npm run seed-admin`, which the boot script runs on every start.
# It is idempotent: it resets the password and re-grants admin when the row
# already exists, so these credentials always work after a boot.
#
# A dot-less domain is intentional — it is unroutable, so this account cannot
# collide with a real one, and it still passes the login form's type="email"
# validation (the HTML spec makes the dotted part optional).
INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@localhost
INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD=mixos-local-dev
